A description of the later AI UK game engine encoding.

The basic design is superficially similar to the earlier games. Various
technologies like the token based compressor and the bitmap graphics are
reused, with the Seas Of Blood style graphics engine.

This file started as Alan Cox's working notes. It has been brought in line
with what the interpreter in this directory does; where the two disagreed,
the interpreter (which is tested against the original games) wins. Things
that the interpreter does not depend on are marked "(notes)" and are
unverified. Memory addresses were checked in 48K and 128K snapshots of the
Spectrum releases.


Engine variants

The interpreter tells four variants of the engine apart (GameVersion in
taylor.h):

Questprobe 3	Questprobe featuring The Human Torch and The Thing.
		The oldest, with its own opcode numbering, text
		encoding and picture format. See the last section.
Rebel Planet	End-of-text markers live inside the token table.
Blizzard Pass	Spectrum 128 only. Action 12 is GOBY.
He-Man		He-Man, Temple of Terror and Kayleth. Second message
		bank, compressed picture scripts, more actions.

Everything below describes the three later variants unless it says
otherwise.


Flags

There are 128 flags, and 256 object location slots. Flags range in value
from 0 to 255. ADD stops at 255 and SUB at 0. Several flags have special
roles.

Flag 0		Location
Flag 1		Set means dark		(Starts 0)
Flag 2		Carried Room		(Starts 253)
Flag 3		Worn Room		(Starts 254)
Flag 4		Max carried
Flag 5		Carried Count		(Starts 0)
Flag 6		Number of objects
Flag 7		Wait counter (Rebel Planet and Kayleth)

Flags 0 to 6 are loaded from the flag table in the game file when a game
starts. The rest start at zero. For Kayleth the interpreter ignores flag 6
and uses 120 objects.


Text

All text is stored tokenised. Each token is stored as ascii with the top
bit used to mark last character. A string is a list of token numbers, one
byte each, ending in 5E or 7E. 5E means that a space is pending after the
string, 7E that none is. Capitalisation is automatic following ".".

In the Rebel Planet variant the 5E and 7E end markers are not bytes of
the string. They are characters inside the token expansions, so finding
the nth string means expanding every token of the strings before it.

The token table is found by searching the file for a known piece of it,
not from a fixed address.


Messages

Stored in the message table in Text format. The first messages are system
used as follows

0	. You can see		(object listing)
1-10	Directions
11	That input contained no recognized words
12	It is beyond my power to do that.
13	. You can go
14	what do you want to do now		(not early games)
15	you're carrying too much.
16	you are carrying the following:
17	nothing at all
18	game's now over. Do you want to play again (Y/N) ?
19	Ok.
20	press any key to continue
21	you already have it
22	sorry, I don't see it here.
23	you don't have it
24	you can't go that way
25	It is dark. You cannot see.
26	do you want to restore a saved game ?
27
28	you are not wearing it
29	you are wearing it
30	(worn),
31	No help at this stage.
32	I don't understand that verb
33+ game text

The He-Man variant has a second message table, printed by action 12.


Objects

Object descriptions are a table of strings in Text format, one per
object. The first objects are "low" objects: their text is written as a
continuation of the room description. The rest are listed after message
0. The file does not say where the low objects end. The interpreter takes
the first object whose description ends in a comma as the first ordinary
one. In Blizzard Pass that does not work and the count is fixed at 70.

Locations
0xFC	destroyed
0xFD	default carried
0xFE	default worn

The carried and worn locations are whatever flags 2 and 3 hold.


Exits

Exit data is in the form

	<bit7 + room number>
		<verb><location> repeated until a code with the top bit is
		found. The top bit indicates we passed the end.
	<0xFF> marks the final end

Verbs 1-10 are exits. The verb number is also the number of the system
message that names the direction.


Pictures

Each picture script is delimited by an 0xFF end marker. These scripts
compose pictures out of block pictures which are themselves encoded in the
same way as the earlier games. In fact the scripts of pictures first appear
in Seas of Blood. Games prior to Robin of Sherwood use display sized
pictures, Robin of Sherwood is special using strips for forest and special
rules.

The picture is 32 by 12 character cells. Script n belongs to room n. In
the He-Man variant the scripts are stored compressed, with a table of
repeated patterns.

The script interpreter is ../aiukgraphics/taylordraw.c, shared with the
Seas of Blood interpreter. The hexadecimal number after each code is the
address of its routine in the Spectrum release of He-Man, where the
dispatcher is at 6F09 and a script is run by calling 6EF9.

Drawing Codes:

0xFF		End
0xFE		733C Mirror left half
0xFD		6FF5 arg1 arg2 Replace colour arg1 with arg2
0xFC		76D4 y x height attribute width Set attributes of a
		rectangle
		Rebel Planet and Blizzard Pass: x y attribute length
		Set attributes of a run of cells
0xFB		700D Make picture attribute area bright
0xFA		7512 Flip image horizontally
0xF9		6F26 arg1 Draw the picture of location arg1 as a part
		of this one (handled in the dispatcher: calls 6EF9)
0xF8		729D arg1 Stop drawing here if object arg1 is not present
		Rebel Planet and Blizzard Pass: arg1 arg2 x y Draw block
		arg2 at x y if object arg1 is present
0xF7		743A block x y }
0xF6		744E block x y } Draw block at x y. The original first
0xF5		7444 block x y } stores 0x0C, 4 or 8 at 6FB7 (an ordinary
				 block draw stores 0). Nothing reads
				 that byte.
0xF4		7458 arg1 Stop drawing here if object arg1 IS present
0xF3		7409 Mirror top half vertically
0xF2		7331 y x y2 width Mirror area horizontally
0xF1		73FE x y y2 width Mirror area vertically
0xEE		7507 y x y2 width Flip area horizontally
0xED		7654 Flip image vertically
0xEC		7649 x y y2 width Flip area vertically
0xEB		0000 CRASH: jumps to the ROM reset (the interpreter
0xEA		0000 stops drawing)
0xE9		7678 arg1 arg2 ..
		arg1 = colour to match
		arg2 = new colour
		Swap colour (paper or ink)
0xE8		747A
		Clear the picture

Any other value, including 0xF0 and 0xEF, is the number of a block
picture and is followed by x and y (6F84, which calls 7234 to find the
block).

"Present" in 0xF8 and 0xF4 means that the object is in the room.


